    <!-- =====================================================
         POPE ST. PIUS X
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="article-section section-parchment">

      <div class="article-inner">

        <p class="section-label">
          Recognition • 1905
        </p>

        <h2>
          A Mission Recognized by Pope St. Pius X
        </h2>

        <p>
          By the early twentieth century,
          Father Müller's charitable work
          had attracted attention beyond
          Mangalore.
        </p>

        <p>
          His institutions provided
          assistance to people of
          different religious backgrounds
          and social circumstances,
          reflecting the Christian charity
          that guided his missionary vocation.
        </p>

        <p>
          In 1905, his work received
          a particularly meaningful
          acknowledgment.
        </p>

        <p>
          According to a contemporary
          report published in the
          December 1905 issue of
          <em> Woodstock Letters </em>,{" "}
          <strong>
            Pope St. Pius X expressed
            admiration for Father Müller's
            charitable undertakings
            and extended his apostolic blessing
          </strong>
          {" "}to Father Müller,
          Dr. L. P. Fernandes,
          their assistants, benefactors,
          and patients.
        </p>

        <p>
          For a Jesuit missionary who
          had spent decades serving
          the sick and poor, this
          acknowledgment was a
          significant moment in the
          history of his institutions.
        </p>

        <p>
          It also illustrates how a
          charitable undertaking that
          began with a small chest
          of medicines had developed
          into a mission recognized
          far beyond the community
          where it began.
        </p>

        <a
          class="inline-source"
          href={sources.woodstock1905}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          Read the December 1905 Woodstock Letters ↗
        </a>

      </div>

    </section>


        <!-- =====================================================
         HAHNEMANN CONNECTION
         HISTORICAL DOCUMENT LEFT — TEXT RIGHT
         ===================================================== -->

    <section class="article-section side-document-section">

      <div class="side-layout two-column-left">

        <!-- =================================================
             HISTORICAL DOCUMENT — LEFT COLUMN
             ================================================= -->

        <figure class="side-image side-left wide-scan">

          <a
            href={images.hahnemann}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            aria-label="View historical publication discussing Father Müller's possible family connection to Samuel Hahnemann"
          >
            <img
              src={images.hahnemann}
              alt="Page from Guide to Health Through Homoeopathy describing a possible ancestral connection between Father Augustus Müller and Samuel Hahnemann's teacher"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </a>

          <figcaption>

            <strong>
              An Intriguing Historical Tradition
            </strong>

            <span>
              <em>Guide to Health Through Homoeopathy</em>,
              p. vii. The publication preserves
              a claim concerning an ancestor
              of Father Müller and
              Samuel Hahnemann.
            </span>

            <a
              href={sources.guide}
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              View Digitized Publication ↗
            </a>

          </figcaption>

        </figure>


        <!-- =================================================
             ARTICLE TEXT — RIGHT COLUMN
             ================================================= -->

        <div class="reading-column">

          <p class="section-label">
            An Unresolved Historical Question
          </p>

          <h2>
            A Possible Family Connection
            to Samuel Hahnemann
          </h2>

          <p>
            Among the more unusual details
            preserved in the history of
            Father Müller's institutions
            is a possible family connection
            to {" "}
            <strong> 
              Samuel Hahnemann (1755–1843), 
            </strong>
            {" "}the founder of homeopathy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The account appears in{" "}
            <em>
              Guide to Health Through Homoeopathy,
            </em>
            {" "}published by Father Muller's
            Charitable Institutions:
          </p>


          <!-- HISTORICAL QUOTATION -->

          <div class="primary-quote">

            <span class="quote-mark">“</span>

            <blockquote>
              It is a very striking and strange
              coincidence that a grand-parent
              of his, one Augustine Muller,
              happened to be the teacher of
              Master Samuel Hahnemann,
              Founder of Homoeopathy,
              in school.
            </blockquote>

            <p>
              — <em>Guide to Health Through Homoeopathy</em>,
              p. vii
            </p>

          </div>


          <p>
            This statement raises a
            fascinating historical question.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hahnemann attended the
            prestigious St. Afra school
            in Meissen, Germany,
            where one of his teachers was{" "}
            <strong>
              Johann August Müller (1731–1804).
            </strong>
          </p>

          <p>
            Historical accounts of
            Hahnemann's education
            independently establish
            the relationship between
            the young student and
            his teacher.
          </p>

          <p>
            Could the “Augustine Muller”
            mentioned in the institutional
            history refer to
            Johann August Müller?
          </p>

          <p>
            The similarity is intriguing.
            However, an important distinction
            must be made.
          </p>


          <!-- HISTORICAL RESEARCH NOTE -->

          <div class="research-note">

            <span class="research-label">
              Historical Research Note
            </span>

            <p>
              The relationship between
              Samuel Hahnemann and
              Johann August Müller
